      "evidence": "The theorem covers F=R or C. Its floating-point dependency defines a floating-point system as a set F contained in R, and its cited dot-product theorem assumes x,y in R^n. No componentwise complex floating-point model or complex dot-product error theorem is assumed, so the cited facts do not license the asserted complex case.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "75a674f1c007204c6ecfd3217e5e49fe64d98b382b1ad98e25af998387be45a5"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:4",
      "id": "thm-gaussian-elimination-with-partial-pivoting-computes-a-nearby-factorisation-with-pivot-growth-bound",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 gives P-hat A+E=L-hat U-hat, then step 2.1 sets Delta A=P-hat^T E and claims that “|Delta A_ij| has the same bound as |E_ij| entrywise.” A nontrivial permutation instead gives |Delta A_ij|=|E_{pi(i),j}|. Thus the entrywise bound must have its row factor permuted, namely |Delta A| <= gamma_n P-hat^T(|L-hat||U-hat|), not the Statement's unpermuted row-i bound.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "75a674f1c007204c6ecfd3217e5e49fe64d98b382b1ad98e25af998387be45a5"
    },
    {
