      "evidence": "The statement includes complex matrices. However, def-standard-relative-floating-point-model-and-unit-roundoff defines rounding only for a finite set contained in R, and thm-floating-point-dot-product-componentwise-and-normwise-error-bounds is explicitly for real vectors. No complex floating-point arithmetic assumption is supplied for the claimed complex QR result.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "877176a7e161e0e711b8d21b8ec4e4e36aab3cb849c417d79b2cb41d3673652a"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:6",
      "id": "thm-householder-qr-is-backward-stable-in-the-standard-floating-point-model",
      "location": "3.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 has A^(k+1)=H-hat_k(A^(k)+E_k), so after the first step A^(1) already includes the nonzero local rounding perturbation E_0. Step 3.1 nevertheless says every A^(k) is obtained from a trailing block of A only by unitary transformations and deletions, and concludes ||A^(k)||_F <= ||A||_F. That does not follow in the floating-point model: prior E_j terms remain in the active block. This unsupported bound is essential to the displayed 3n gamma_m estimate.",
